            <div xml:id="d666">

                <pb facs="0791" n="679" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg679"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12997</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3402</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-01">March 1, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received March
                        23.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Transportation of Chinese Troops on American Vessels
                    on the Upper Yangtze</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Legation’s
                    despatch No. 3373 of February 19, 1935,<anchor n="86" xml:id="d666-f1"/> in
                    regard to the above-mentioned subject, and to enclose for the information of the
                    Department a copy of despatch No. 53, dated February 25, 1935, from the Consul
                    General at Hankow.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d666-f2"/></p>
                <p>Mr. Josselyn reports in this despatch that no Chinese troops have been
                    transported to Szechwan Province by American vessels, but that one American
                    vessel carried a certain amount of ammunition to that region.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. E.
                        Gauss</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of
                    Legation</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
